===Etymology 1===
[[File:Standard deviation diagram.svg|thumb|Standard deviation chart with the '''average''' at the center]]
{{ety|en|id=central tendency|:af|frm:avarie<ety:inh<fro:avarie>>|-age|tree=1}}

{{unc|en|title=Not entirely certain.}} The oldest meaning in English is {{m-g|customs duty}}. Borrowed from {{bor|en|frm|avarie|t=damage to ship or cargo}}, from {{der|en|fro|avarie}}, from {{der|en|roa-oit|avaria}} where it is first attested in the 12th century in the context of Mediterranean trade. From there most sources trace it to {{der|en|ar|عَوَارِيَّة|t=damaged goods}}, from {{m|ar|عَوَار|t=fault, blemish, defect, flaw}}, from {{m|ar|عَوِرَ|t=to lose an eye}},<ref>{{cite-book|en|author=John Ayto|title=Word Origins|entry=average|year=2005|edition=2nd|year_published=1990|publisher=A & C Black|location=London|page=43|isbn=0-7136-7498-9}}</ref><ref>{{R:en:ODEE|entry=average|page=64|column=2}}</ref> but the OED gives it a Romance derivation from {{der|en|it|avere|t=property, goods}} or the like.<ref>{{R:OED Online|average|n2|3332791542}}</ref>

The English suffix {{m|en|-age}} was added in {{glossary|analogy}} to words like {{m|en|damage}}.

